WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.760
Vsauce! Kevin here -- Jennifer Thompson&nbsp;
studied every detail of her attacker's face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.680
as she was being sexually assaulted at&nbsp;
knifepoint. His hairline, broad cheekbones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.360
a mustache that looked like a pencil-thin dirt&nbsp;
smear. She couldn't fight back physically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:23.520
but MENTALLY she could memorize every&nbsp;
little detail and bring him to justice.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.200
Police described Jennifer as&nbsp;
the best witness they'd ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.720
had. Her eyewitness testimony convicted&nbsp;
Ronald Cotton, and he was sentenced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:39.040
life in prison plus 50 years despite no&nbsp;
physical evidence linking him to the crime.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:46.480
There was no evidence because he didn't do it&nbsp;
-- and 11 years later, DNA testing proved it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:53.200
So how could the "best witness ever"&nbsp;
get it completely wrong? Why do about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:59.360
70% of DNA exonerations in the US&nbsp;
involve eyewitness misidentification?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:06.080
And how can what we've seen turn&nbsp;
into something we never saw?

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.280
[intro]

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.200
I need you to… point to the shape that rolled&nbsp;
over your foot on the evening of March 14th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:25.280
2015. Okay, this example seems stupid, but it&nbsp;
shows one of the many, many problems with witness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:32.080
identification. Only one shape in the lineup&nbsp;
comes close to the description of the suspect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:38.800
the choice is obvious. Only a circle&nbsp;
can roll! Now this lineup requires an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.240
actual identification of size and color.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:48.800
But here’s a question. How do you even get&nbsp;
to a lineup? First, you need a description.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.800
Jennifer Thompson studied pages of ears, chins,&nbsp;
and noses so police could reconstruct her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:01.280
attacker's face using an IdentiKit. In&nbsp;
1984, that was a sketch on paper -- now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:07.680
the IdentiKit is digital. The hand-drawn facial&nbsp;
composite looked nearly perfect to her. Now the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.240
police had a picture of the suspect that&nbsp;
could be broadcast on the local news.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.760
Here’s the problem.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.680
Some witnesses are amazing -- their&nbsp;
autobiographical memory of an event is flawless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:26.480
But then there’s the rest of us, and Hugo&nbsp;
Münsterberg wrote the book on how what we forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:33.040
and what we don’t know becomes an&nbsp;
illusion that replaces reality.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:38.720
In 1908, Münsterberg published “On the Witness&nbsp;
Stand,” a collection of articles and essays about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:45.600
psychological factors that affect trial outcomes.&nbsp;
What’s more persuasive than a witness pointing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:52.880
a defendant and swearing they did it? Münsterberg&nbsp;
insisted THAT gold standard of evidence in a trial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:58.880
was actually… really untrustworthy.&nbsp;
Trials pre-date the ancient Greeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:06.480
and while we’ve always known witnesses could lie,&nbsp;
only recently do we understand how easy it is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.920
THINK you’re telling the truth&nbsp;
when your brain is just… wrong.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:18.160
Münsterberg suggested that justice often&nbsp;
miscarried due to the “treachery of human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:25.280
memory” -- and to prove it, he pointed the&nbsp;
finger at himself. He wasn’t just some academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:33.120
pontificating on criminal justice theory -- he&nbsp;
was robbed, and as a witness, he messed it all up.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:40.000
It went like this: He testified under oath that&nbsp;
burglars entered his house through a window. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:47.040
actually came in through the cellar. He said they&nbsp;
left a clock packed in paper. It was wrapped in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:54.800
tablecloth. He gave a complete list of garments&nbsp;
that were stolen; it turns out they took 7 more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:01.840
than he said. He claimed he found candle wax on&nbsp;
the 2nd floor… but the wax drippings were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:10.000
in the attic. Finally, he reported 2 burglars.&nbsp;
But maybe it was just 1 -- he had no way to tell.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:17.920
Münsterberg had given over 3,000 academic lectures&nbsp;
and not once had to use note cards. His mind and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:25.440
memory were amazing. But he admitted that he’d&nbsp;
rushed into the house and surveyed it all quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:32.800
noting after the fact that his brain filled in&nbsp;
the gaps for him. The burglar was eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.320
caught breaking into another house, and they&nbsp;
found Münsterberg’s jacket in his apartment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:46.880
But the fact is -- the world’s expert on witness&nbsp;
reliability -- was almost completely wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:53.520
He said, “I felt myself somewhat alarmed&nbsp;
in seeing how many illusions had come in.”

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.000
So…

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.840
If the pioneer of witness reliability&nbsp;
scholarship couldn't overcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:08.320
his illusions, what chance did a college student&nbsp;
traumatized by a violent sexual assault have?

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.880
Police presented Jennifer Thompson with a photo&nbsp;
lineup of 6 men. They told her that the suspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.240
might not even be in the lineup -- but she&nbsp;
figured if he wasn’t there, why would they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:27.440
bother? She recognized one of the photos and&nbsp;
chose Ronald Cotton with absolute certainty.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:33.600
A week later, Jennifer recognized the same man in&nbsp;
a physical lineup. To her, the parts that didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:40.480
match her memory made him even guiltier -- like&nbsp;
he’d changed his hair to purposely deceive her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:48.000
The photo lineup reinforced her mental image&nbsp;
of her attacker, and anti-proof became proof.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:54.480
Police confirmed that she’d chosen the&nbsp;
same man in both lineups. She said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.440
"It was him. There was no doubt in my mind."

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.800
Did you feel absolutely certain? Absolutely&nbsp;
certain. Well, what was said to me afterwards was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.920
“That’s the same person you picked out in&nbsp;
the photo lineup.” So in my mind I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.200
“Bingo! I did it right!”

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.320
So if that’s the case… How can&nbsp;
the truth compete with that?

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:23.920
“Estimator variables” are the parts of eyewitness&nbsp;
identification that police can’t control. So like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:31.120
cross-racial ID’s tend to be less accurate&nbsp;
than ID’s within a witness’s race. That isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:36.720
the police’s fault. People think different&nbsp;
things, and they just plain see different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:45.600
things -- from an optical illusion to the colors&nbsp;
of a dress. But the real enemy of memory is time.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:50.400
Hermann Ebbinghaus’s forgetting curve&nbsp;
provides a visual representation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.600
of how quickly your memory can&nbsp;
erode -- and as early as the 1880’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:02.160
his equation expressed the vulnerability of&nbsp;
memory over time. But forgetting over time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:08.080
is only half the problem -- the other&nbsp;
half is teaching yourself the lie.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:14.800
Every time you re-tell your story to police,&nbsp;
to a lawyer, to a journalist, to a friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:20.000
you’re essentially engaging in&nbsp;
the spaced repetition of practice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:26.400
You’re learning. Your memories get clearer,&nbsp;
almost like memorizing lines for a play.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.880
So the longer it’s been, the more&nbsp;
you forget. The more you forget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:38.560
the more you invent. The more you repeat, the more&nbsp;
confident you are -- whether it’s true or not.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:42.560
Jennifer Thompson said, “If&nbsp;
there was a death penalty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:49.200
I wanted Ronald Cotton to get it. I would pull&nbsp;
the switch if they let me.” When she pointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:55.840
her finger at Cotton to identify him during the&nbsp;
trial, she said she wished it had been a gun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:02.480
Ronald Cotton had become her attacker in&nbsp;
her mind through unconscious transference.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:08.240
But there was no physical evidence -- no&nbsp;
fingerprints, no clothing, no fibers from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:14.160
the victim’s apartment -- linking Ronald Cotton&nbsp;
to the crime. He maintained his innocence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:21.120
refused to entertain a plea bargain. He was&nbsp;
convicted based largely on Jennifer’s testimony.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:29.040
3 years later, Jennifer Thompson came face&nbsp;
to face with her actual attacker -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:31.120
didn’t even notice.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:37.840
Ronald Cotton was given a new trial -- that’s&nbsp;
great, right? WRONG -- this time he also faced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:44.800
charges for another assault and burglary from&nbsp;
that same night. Meanwhile, he’d met a man in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:51.760
prison who looked a lot like Jennifer’s original&nbsp;
composite sketch -- and he looked so similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:58.080
Ronald Cotton that other inmates couldn’t tell&nbsp;
them apart. Bobby Poole lived in the same town as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:04.080
Ronald and Jennifer, and he was currently serving&nbsp;
a sentence for sexual assault. If all of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:10.400
wasn’t enough to raise suspicions, he’d literally&nbsp;
confessed to other inmates that he assaulted her.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:16.640
Ronald’s lawyers called Bobby Poole&nbsp;
as a witness in the new trial. Poole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:22.960
denied everything. Jennifer Thompson had wanted&nbsp;
to be the one to personally execute Ronald Cotton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:29.840
but when she was in the same courtroom as her&nbsp;
real attacker, the man whose face she’d studied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:38.720
she didn’t recognize him at all. Ronald was&nbsp;
convicted again and sent back to prison.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:45.440
Richard Rosen, a professor at UNC-Chapel Hill,&nbsp;
and lawyer Tom Lambeth took on Ronald’s case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:51.760
They secured the DNA analysis that wasn’t&nbsp;
available in 1984. Ronald insisted that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:58.400
he'd be cleared -- and the stakes were&nbsp;
high. A DNA match would cement his guilt.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:06.880
Ronald wasn’t lying. The tests showed that not&nbsp;
only was Ronald innocent, but that Bobby Poole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:14.800
was the attacker -- and over 10 years after&nbsp;
first being arrested, Ronald Cotton was free.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:22.640
Jennifer didn’t dispute the DNA findings. She&nbsp;
knew she’d been proven wrong. She wasn’t defensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:29.040
about it; she was horrified. She said, “I had&nbsp;
to tell people not only was I was a rape victim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:35.360
but a rape victim with an extremely bad memory&nbsp;
and someone had to pay 11 years for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.440
I wondered how I could have been so stupid.”

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:47.440
Jennifer was not stupid. During the attack she&nbsp;
actually convinced Poole to get rid of his knife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:52.800
then outwitted him by offering to get him a&nbsp;
drink and using the distraction to escape.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:59.120
She just fell prey to the&nbsp;
vulnerabilities all witnesses have and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:05.200
a few simple changes can drastically&nbsp;
improve witness reliability.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:12.400
Lineups that offer a range of similar choices&nbsp;
are most effective -- that way suspects can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:20.240
stand out. And making lineups sequential, so one&nbsp;
photo at a time is compared to a witness’s memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:24.160
instead of being compared to one&nbsp;
another, is even more effective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:30.560
Police need to make it clear to witnesses that&nbsp;
the suspect might not be there -- and the cops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:35.920
who make the lineup shouldn’t be the ones who&nbsp;
administer it. That ‘double blind’ process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:41.840
ensures police can’t give any verbal or non-verbal&nbsp;
cues to the witness to influence their choice.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:45.360
Recording the lineup solidifies the record,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:50.560
and police should ask witnesses to detail&nbsp;
their level of confidence in their choice.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:56.720
But Jennifer Thompson would’ve&nbsp;
said 100%, and advancing science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:00.880
was the only thing that saved&nbsp;
Ronald Cotton from a life in prison.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:05.240
“In my mind, I was sure of what I saw. Um.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:11.840
Thinking back on it, uh, I don’t know&nbsp;
that I would’ve done anything different.”

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:18.960
Jennifer and Ronald are friends now.&nbsp;
He never blamed her for being wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:21.920
They’ve given talks together nationwide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:27.680
their families are close, and they even end&nbsp;
phone conversations by saying “Love you.”

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:34.560
Half the states in the US have instituted some&nbsp;
form of eyewitness identification reform. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:41.680
can we make sure what happened to both Ronald&nbsp;
Cotton and Jennifer Thompson never happens again?

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:50.000
Probably not. Hugo Münsterberg was right --&nbsp;
from biological limitations to systematic error,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:56.800
the moment our eyes and ears capture&nbsp;
something, the truth is vulnerable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:00.720
We can get better, but we'll never be perfect.

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:08.880
2,200 years ago, the Roman playwright&nbsp;
Plautus said that “1 eyewitness is worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:15.760
10 ear witnesses.” People attest to the truth&nbsp;
by saying they saw something with their own two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:21.760
eyes. They’ll tell you that something is&nbsp;
so good you need to see it to believe it.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:28.080
But the truth is… sometimes you&nbsp;
need to believe it to see it.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:33.840
And as always, thanks for watching.

